## 7. What to Avoid

- **The supplement flat-lay / glowing pill-bottle product shot.** Capsules spilling artfully, moonlit lavender, "natural = leaves and gradients" wellness aesthetics — the exact marketing register the article spends its whole length deflating. The compounds are spec-tagged containers on an audited shelf, never glamour objects.
- **An equal-weight grid of 10+ supplements.** The article's entire intelligence is *unequal* treatment — gate first, four that work, one misfiled, maybes dimmed, one binned. A tidy uniform grid (the default infographic move) erases the leverage ordering and the epistemics. If every item is the same size, the poster has failed the article.
- **Making melatonin the red villain.** The article doesn't say melatonin is dangerous — it says it's *misfiled*. Red belongs to the antihistamine bin alone; melatonin gets the amber reclassification treatment and the clock drawer, which is both more accurate and more interesting.
